[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) was marked **known**: one bug, one fix. Read report by report, it
holds three problems. Engineering closed #4471 on 5 October with two answers, and the third
problem is still open.

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} {issues} {states} />

<Callout kind="warn">
#4471 is closed, but not every report in it is answered. `parser` is fixed
(`fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales`). `hint` won't be fixed because the booking-v3 form is
retired. The **one-day move** was in neither answer and is still open in its own ticket. Its
reports will keep coming after the parser fix.
</Callout>

## The three problems

**`parser`: the shared parser reads dates month first.** Every tenant on file writes the day
first, so any date with a day of 12 or less comes out with day and month swapped. This is the
bug #4471 describes, and it holds most of the reports.

**`hint`: the booking-v3 form contradicts itself.** Its date field prints `MM/DD/YYYY`, but the
form reads what people type the tenant's way, day first. People follow the hint, and their date
gets swapped. It only happens on the booking form of tenants that run `booking-v3`. Their app and
email reports are `parser`.

**`one-day`: the date moves by one day.** These bodies do not swap day and month. The booking
lands a day early, and where you are decides which day you see: one meeting showed as Tuesday in
Lisbon and Monday in Toronto. That points at a time-zone or daylight-saving conversion, not at the
parser. The desk already noted these reports (`oneDayMove`), but the #4471 page counts them under
`parser`.

## How this was counted

- The export has duplicate rows. One id is one report.
- A report's problem comes from its tenant and the way it came in (`causes.ts`), not from the
  intake tag. Intake guesses from the reporter's browser, and it got many of these wrong.
- The one-day moves are the ids in `desk-notes.json`. Every other report that names two dates
  names an exact day/month swap.
- Counts are the desk's own: the desk files the one-day moves under their tenant's cause, so the
  `parser` count includes them, as marked in the table. They are tracked in their own ticket too.
- The table is computed from the data each time the page renders. If the desk merges more
  reports into #4471, the counts follow.

## Why they were merged

[Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges reports whose subject lines match,
without reading the body. All three problems arrive with the same subjects ("Wrong day on the
invite", "Date is wrong"), so they all landed in one ticket. Subject lines say how a problem
looks, not what causes it.

